Original commit body from acb2687647347d89b0b0c2b61ab90a6a7d181110:

Four defects, each of which let a correctly configured two-account
deployment report as healthy while being none of those things.

1. A relative --tracker-root silently disabled the host-protected config.
   Layer 0 keys on the git toplevel containing the config directory, and
   the helper that finds it walks parents -- which for a relative path
   terminates at "." without ever seeing .git. It returned "no repo", and
   load_config treated that identically to "genuinely outside a
   repository" and fell through to the in-repo config, the one file the
   agent can write. The agent controls its own argv, so
   `--tracker-root ./.agent` was a one-flag downgrade of the whole layer.
   The path is resolved before the walk; the legitimate non-git
   fallthrough is kept and now means only what it documents.

2. _detect_shared_group probed the wrong directories. It examined .agent/
   and the repo toplevel, both of which a correct two-user setup leaves at
   the owner's primary group -- the instructions chgrp .agent/tracker* and
   .git. So it reported "single-user" on a properly configured box and the
   three checks gated on it did nothing. The one with teeth creates
   missing tracker directories WITHOUT the shared group or setgid bit,
   leaving a fresh .ops or stealth the other user cannot write.

3. Check #10b set config.yaml to 0444 and check #12's group sweep put
   group-write straight back, landing on 0464. Both reported "fixed" on
   every run, so the wizard never converged despite documenting
   idempotence -- and 0464 in a group containing the agent means the agent
   can write in place whichever config it does not own. Mode for
   config.yaml now belongs solely to #10b.

4. Under host protection the wizard inspected and edited a decoy. Checks
   5-10b and `htrac setup configure` hardcoded the in-repo path, so a
   human on a protected host would validate a file the loader ignores and
   the editor would print "Wrote ... (mode 0444)" having changed no
   governance at all. That is worse than no wizard, because it looks like
   it worked. Every config-reading check now resolves through one helper,
   configure refuses and names the authoritative file, and setup lists the
   leftover in-repo copies for deletion.

Also: setup no longer warns about running as the agent account when a
shared group is present (that is the expected state, and an unclearable
warning is how a report teaches people to stop reading it), and check #7
reports a malformed config instead of dying with an AttributeError
traceback -- a diagnostic is what a human runs precisely when the config
is already wrong.

23 new tests; the group-permission ones use a real secondary group rather
than a patched Path.stat, so the permission arithmetic under test is the
kernel's. Tracker suite 2963 passed, 100% coverage in isolation, mypy
strict ratchet 669 delta +0.

Refs INV-mizid.

